## Brevo:ApiKey fail-fast check doesn't actually run at startup

### Status
Done.

### GitHub Issue
None yet.

### Summary
The `Brevo:ApiKey` validation in `Program.cs` lives inside the `configureClient` delegate passed
to `AddHttpClient<BrevoEmailSender>`, which .NET's typed-client factory only invokes lazily, on
the first `HttpClient` creation for that typed client — not during application startup, despite
the surrounding comment implying startup-time validation.

### Motivation
A deploy with a missing or blank `Brevo:ApiKey` currently starts up successfully with no error.
The `InvalidOperationException` only fires the first time a real request resolves
`BrevoEmailSender` — e.g. the first user registration in production — surfacing as an unhandled
exception on that request instead of a controlled startup failure, and silently breaking every
registration until someone notices. This also re-reads the config value and re-mutates the
default request headers on every `HttpClient` handed out for this typed client, not just once.

### Proposed Changes
Move the `Brevo:ApiKey` read and validation out of the `configureClient` delegate and into
`Program.cs`'s top-level setup code (before/alongside the `AddHttpClient<BrevoEmailSender>` call),
so it runs unconditionally during startup. Pass the validated key into the client configuration
from that already-validated value rather than re-reading configuration inside the delegate.

### Acceptance Criteria
- A missing or blank `Brevo:ApiKey` throws during application startup, before the app begins
  accepting requests — not on the first email send.
- The configuration read and header setup happen once, not on every `HttpClient` resolution.

### Out of Scope
- Any other configuration value's fail-fast timing — scoped to `Brevo:ApiKey` only.

