## Manage/Email.cshtml.cs still hand-rolls its own confirmation-email sequence

### Status
Done.

### GitHub Issue
None yet.

### Summary
`Email.cshtml.cs`'s `OnPostSendVerificationEmailAsync` still inlines its own copy of
"generate confirmation token → base64url-encode → build `ConfirmEmail` callback URL → send" —
the exact sequence `EmailConfirmationLinkSender` was extracted to consolidate — leaving a fourth,
un-migrated copy alongside `Register.cshtml.cs`, `ResendEmailConfirmation.cshtml.cs`, and
`ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs`, which were all migrated to the shared service.

### Motivation
`REFACTOR-signin-manager-and-confirmation-email.ospec`'s stated goal was removing duplicated
copies of this sequence so a future change to the confirmation-link shape (URL format, subject
line, token encoding) only has to be made in one place. This call site is untouched, so a future
change to `EmailConfirmationLinkSender` can silently miss it, leaving the "Resend verification
email" button on the Manage/Email page inconsistent with the other three flows — and this drift
wouldn't be caught by `EmailConfirmationLinkSenderTests`, since this code path doesn't go through
that class at all.

### Proposed Changes
Update `Email.cshtml.cs`'s `OnPostSendVerificationEmailAsync` to call
`EmailConfirmationLinkSender.SendAsync` (injecting it the same way `Register.cshtml.cs` and
`ResendEmailConfirmation.cshtml.cs` do) instead of its own inline token/URL/send sequence.

### Acceptance Criteria
- `OnPostSendVerificationEmailAsync` calls `EmailConfirmationLinkSender.SendAsync` rather than
  reimplementing the sequence inline.
- `EmailConfirmationLinkSender` is the single place confirmation-email links are generated and
  sent, across all four call sites (`Register`, `ResendEmailConfirmation`, `ExternalLogin`,
  `Manage/Email`).
- Existing behavior of the "Resend verification email" button is unchanged from the user's
  perspective (same email content, same redirect).

### Out of Scope
- `ForgotPassword.cshtml.cs`'s password-reset email — a different email/flow, not a copy of the
  confirmation-link sequence.

