## Silent bounce to home page on a concurrent-mutation race in IsNotAllowed branches

### Status
Done.

### GitHub Issue
None yet.

### Summary
`Login.cshtml.cs`'s and `ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs`'s `IsNotAllowed` branches each re-look-up the
user (by name / by external login, respectively) after sign-in already succeeded internally, in
order to get the email for the `RegisterConfirmation` redirect, and pass the result straight into
`RedirectToPage("./RegisterConfirmation", new { email = user?.Email })` with no null guard. If
that second lookup doesn't find the same account (e.g. deleted or renamed between the two calls),
`email` comes through `null`, and `RegisterConfirmationModel.OnGetAsync` silently redirects to
`/Index` with no error message or logging.

### Motivation
A narrow, low-likelihood race, but a nameable one with no test coverage: a user whose credentials
were just verified correct gets silently bounced to the homepage with zero explanation instead of
any error message — a dropped error path relative to how explicitly other failure cases in these
same two files are signaled.

### Proposed Changes
In both `Login.cshtml.cs`'s and `ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs`'s `IsNotAllowed` branches, guard against
a `null` result from the re-lookup: if the user can't be found, show a generic error (e.g. via
`ModelState`/`TempData`) instead of redirecting to `RegisterConfirmation` with a null email.
`RegisterConfirmationModel.OnGetAsync` should likewise treat a missing/empty `email` query
parameter as an explicit error case rather than a silent redirect, so a caller reaching it any
other way (not just this race) fails visibly.

### Acceptance Criteria
- If the post-sign-in user re-lookup fails to find an account in either `Login.cshtml.cs` or
  `ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs`, the user sees a generic error rather than being silently redirected
  to `/Index`.
- `RegisterConfirmationModel.OnGetAsync` with a missing/empty `email` parameter surfaces an
  explicit error rather than silently redirecting to `/Index`.

### Out of Scope
- Actually preventing the race (e.g. via a transaction) — out of scope; this only covers making
  the failure visible instead of silent.

